It has come to our attention that some of the unofficial binaries
for Amiga NetHack 3.1 that are floating around the various networks
are not compiled correctly and, in some cases, have misleading or
erroneous statements in the accompanying documentation.  While we
have no objection to people creating their own binaries (in accordance
with the license), I feel it's in everyone's best interest to
explain on some things that evidently were not sufficiently well
understood by some of those people.

The official binaries are available for anonymous ftp from
linc.cis.upenn.edu (130.91.6.8) in pub/NH3.1/binaries/amiga.  The
sources (complete, other rumors not withstanding) are also available on
that system in pub/NH3.1/sources.  Please restrict transfers to
non-business hours (USA east coast time).

The distribution consists of two .lzh files and a file called readmefirst.
The sizes of the two .lzh files are:
	415045 NH31P0-1.lzh
	377367 NH31P0-2.lzh

Floppy support - Amiga NetHack 3.1 will run on a floppy based system.  It
isn't as easy as with a hard drive, but it does work.

"Hard disk" version vs "floppy disk" version - there's no such thing.  One
set of binaries will work on either.  The confusion here is due to the
presence of a feature we call "split binaries."  As NetHack is more than
one meg when first compiled it will not fit on a single Amiga floppy.
We have addressed this by shipping split binaries.

"It says that starting the second game should be faster than the first
but it's not." - again, this is a split binaries issue.

To tell if your version is running with split binaries, check for the
following files (as shipped, you may have moved them during installation):
 HackExe:NetHack.dir
 HackExe:NetHack.c00
 HackExe:NetHack.c01
 HackExe:NetHack.d00
If you have a file called simply "NetHack" you do not have the official
distribution.

If you are not "running split" than whenever a game starts up it must
load the entire >1 meg file - this takes about 60 seconds on my system.
If you are running split, the loader will load only the modifiable data
for the second game - it re-uses the code and the non-modifiable data -
this takes about 6 seconds on my system.  (Note that this means that
the split binaries are more than just hacking the original binary into
a couple pieces).

I hope this clears up some of the confusion.
Ken Lorber
(for the Amiga NetHack Team)
